  <!-- ==================== WHAT IS ==================== -->
  <h2 id="what-is">What is DIGIPIN?</h2>
  <p>DIGIPIN — the <b>Digital Postal Index Number</b> — is a nationwide, geo-coded addressing grid developed by the
    <b>Department of Posts (India Post)</b> in collaboration with <b>IIT Hyderabad</b>, with support from
    <b>NRSC (ISRO)</b>. It is designed as a <b>Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI)</b> for India: an open, free,
    standard layer that both government and private services can build on — the foundation of
    "Address as a Service" <span class="source-tag">(source: India Post)</span>.</p>
  <p>The Department released the DIGIPIN grid for public consultation as a beta and, after stakeholder feedback,
    finalised the grid. Because DIGIPIN is derived purely from coordinates, it is <b>permanent</b>: new buildings,
    renamed roads and changed boundaries do not change a location's DIGIPIN, and no personal information is stored
    in the code.</p>

  <h2 id="digits">How Many Digits Does a DIGIPIN Have?</h2>
  <p>A full DIGIPIN has exactly <b>10 characters</b>, written in three groups: <b>XXX-YYY-ZZZZ</b>. Each character
    comes from a set of <b>16 symbols</b> — the digits 2 to 9 and the letters C, F, J, K, L, M, P, T:</p>
  <div class="symbol-strip">
    <span>2</span><span>3</span><span>4</span><span>5</span><span>6</span><span>7</span><span>8</span><span>9</span>
    <span>C</span><span>F</span><span>J</span><span>K</span><span>L</span><span>M</span><span>P</span><span>T</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="based-on">What Are DIGIPIN Codes Based On?</h2>
  <p>DIGIPIN codes are based purely on <b>geography</b>. India's territory — latitude 2.5°N to 38.5°N and
    longitude 63.5°E to 99.5°E, including maritime regions — is divided into a <b>hierarchical 4×4 grid, 10 levels
    deep</b>. Each level picks one of 16 symbols (labelled in an anticlockwise spiral) and narrows the square down,
    from about 1000 km at the first character to about 3.8 m at the tenth. The first characters even give the code
    built-in directionality — neighbouring cells get neighbouring symbols.</p>

  <h2 id="precision">DIGIPIN Precision Levels</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Code length</th><th>Grid size</th><th>Approx. area</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 character</td><td>~9°</td><td>~1000 km</td><td>Country / region level</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 characters</td><td>~33.75′</td><td>~62.5 km</td><td>District level</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6 characters</td><td>~0.53′</td><td>~1 km</td><td>Neighbourhood level</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 characters</td><td>~0.03′</td><td>~60 m</td><td>Building level</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>10 characters (full)</b></td><td>~0.12″</td><td><b>~3.8 m</b></td><td><b>Precise location</b></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">Source: India Post DIGIPIN Technical Document — each additional character multiplies precision 4×.</p>

  <!-- ==================== VS PIN ==================== -->
  <h2 id="vs-pincode">DIGIPIN vs PIN Code — What's the Difference?</h2>
  <p>This is the most common confusion: a <b>PIN code</b> (Postal Index Number, the classic 6-digit code) identifies a
    <b>post office delivery area</b> — often covering several neighbourhoods and thousands of addresses. A
    <b>DIGIPIN</b> identifies <b>one precise 4m × 4m spot</b> on the ground. They solve different problems:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>PIN Code</th><th>DIGIPIN</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>What it identifies</b></td><td>A post office delivery zone</td><td>One exact 4m × 4m location</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td>6 digits (e.g. 110001)</td><td>10 symbols, XXX-YYY-ZZZZ (e.g. 39J-438-TJC7)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Based on</b></td><td>Postal delivery routes</td><td>Latitude & longitude grid</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Precision</b></td><td>Area level (km)</td><td>~3.8 metres</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Changes over time</b></td><td>Can be revised by India Post</td><td>Permanent — derived from coordinates</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Needs internet?</b></td><td>No</td><td>No (pure computation, works offline)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>DIGIPIN does <b>not</b> replace the PIN code — it complements it. Use the PIN code to route a parcel to the right
    post office, and a DIGIPIN to tell the delivery partner the <i>exact</i> doorstep, farmhouse gate, or event spot —
    even where no street address exists. You can search every PIN code of India on our <a href="/">pincode directory homepage</a>.</p>

  <!-- ==================== USES ==================== -->
  <h2 id="uses">What is DIGIPIN Used For?</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="mini"><h3>📦 Delivery & e-commerce</h3><p>Pinpoint exact delivery spots — including villages, farms and new layouts with no formal address. Reduces failed deliveries and last-mile confusion.</p></div>
    <div class="mini"><h3>🚨 Emergency response</h3><p>Share a precise location with ambulance, fire or police services in seconds — a 10-character code instead of long directions.</p></div>
    <div class="mini"><h3>🏦 Banking & KYC</h3><p>Verify addresses precisely; the Department of Posts positions DIGIPIN as infrastructure for "Address as a Service".</p></div>
    <div class="mini"><h3>🚛 Logistics & fleet</h3><p>Standardised geo-reference for routing, serviceability checks and geofencing across India, including maritime zones.</p></div>
    <div class="mini"><h3>🏛️ Government services</h3><p>A common addressing layer for public service delivery, aligned with the National Geospatial Policy.</p></div>
    <div class="mini"><h3>📱 Sharing locations</h3><p>A DIGIPIN is short, human-readable and permanent — easy to share over WhatsApp or print on a visiting card.</p></div>
  </div>

